---
/**
 * The note entry — Note, Web Clipping and Book Note.
 *
 * ── ONE `note` TYPE, THREE VARIANTS, AND ONE SHAPE ───────────────────────────
 *
 * `genre` discriminates the three (CLAUDE.md → the five content types), but the
 * three Figma boards draw ONE layout with one slot filled differently:
 *
 *                       Note 261:2693   Clipping 261:2209   Book Note 261:2806
 *   article band        64 / 64         64 / 64             64 / 64
 *   eyebrow -> h1       8               8                   8
 *   h1 -> prose         16              16                  16
 *   rail (span-3)       empty           source card         cover, 48, card
 *   body -> Topics      64              64                  64
 *
 * So the variant lives entirely in the rail, and everything else here is shared.
 * The rail is the NEXT piece; this file is the shape the three hang off.
 *
 * ── TWO BANDS, AND THE SPLIT IS ABOUT THE RAIL ───────────────────────────────
 *
 * ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 * │  THE RAIL'S TOP EDGE IS THE BODY'S, NOT THE TITLE'S. That is the whole      │
 * │  reason the title sits in a band of its own. Merge them and the rail rides  │
 * │  up to the eyebrow.                                                        │
 * └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
 *
 * The boards are unambiguous once you look at the frame tree rather than the
 * picture: on both, the rail is a CHILD of the same frame as the Prose, so the two
 * share a top edge, and the title block is the frame above it.
 *
 *   Web Clipping 261:2209   title block h128 at y=0   content frame at y=144
 *   Book Note    261:2806   title block h80  at y=0   content frame at y=96
 *
 * ── THIS FILE ARGUED THE OPPOSITE FIRST, AND IT WAS WRONG ────────────────────
 *
 * The first draft was ONE band with the title and body in a shared `--col-main`
 * wrapper, on the reasoning that the article page splits into two bands only so
 * `order` can move its hero between them — machinery a note has no use for. It
 * built, and it put the rail 96px too high (caught by Andy on screen, 2026-09-01).
 *
 * The hero swap is a second thing the split ENABLES, not what it is for. Both
 * detail pages need the same thing for the same reason, so they say it the same
 * way — which is the argument for repeating the structure rather than saving the
 * four declarations a one-band version costs.
 *
 * ── WHAT THE PADDING IS, AND WHY THERE IS NO BREAKPOINT IN IT ────────────────
 *
 * 64 above the title, 16 between title and body, 64 below the content. Split
 * across the two bands as `rhythm-band / rhythm-heading-close` and `0 /
 * rhythm-band`, which is the article page's lg arrangement — the difference being
 * that a note holds it at EVERY width.
 *
 * The article draws 64/16 at lg and 32/32 at 375, because below lg its hero comes
 * between the title and the prose and the title is bound to nothing. Here the title
 * is bound to the prose at 375 exactly as at 1440 — the boards draw 16 at both — so
 * this file contains no `@container` for it. `--rhythm-band` clamps 32 -> 64 on its
 * own, so the outer numbers track the boards without a breakpoint either.
 *
 * ── AND THE REMAINING 64s ARE A ROW GAP ──────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * <Grid>'s `row-gap` is `--rhythm-heading-major` (64), which is every other gap on
 * every board: body -> Topics on desktop, and body -> cover -> card -> Topics on the
 * two mobile boards. So the content band's three grid items produce all of them from
 * one declaration nobody wrote, with the rail's `display: contents` dispersal doing
 * the narrow case.
 */
import type {TypedObject} from 'astro-portabletext/types'
import type {INSIGHT_DETAIL_QUERY_RESULT} from '../../sanity.types'
import Band from './Band.astro'
import Grid from './Grid.astro'
import EntryHeader from './EntryHeader.astro'
import Prose from './prose/Prose.astro'
import TopicList from './TopicList.astro'
import SanityImage from './SanityImage.astro'
import SourceCard from './cards/SourceCard.astro'
import {formatYear} from '../lib/date'

/**
 * The two refs, taken FROM THE GENERATED QUERY TYPE rather than hand-declared.
 *
 * `Extract<…, {_type: 'note'}>` narrows the detail query's union to its note member,
 * which is the shape this component is actually handed. Written out here it would be
 * a second description of the same projection, free to drift the moment a field is
 * added to the query — and drift in the direction that silently types a real field as
 * absent.
 */
type Note = Extract<NonNullable<INSIGHT_DETAIL_QUERY_RESULT>, {_type: 'note'}>
type ClipRef = Note['clipRef']
type BookRef = Note['bookRef']

export interface Props {
  title?: string | null
  genre?: string | null
  /** ISO date string, straight from `pubDate`. */
  date?: string | null
  body?: TypedObject[] | null
  /** Already narrowed to non-null by the page. */
  topics: string[]
  /** The canonical permalink, for `u-url` / `u-uid`. Built by the page from `Astro.site`. */
  permalink: string
  /** A Web Clipping's source. Its PRESENCE selects the rail's contents, not the genre. */
  clip?: ClipRef
  /** A Book Note's source. Exactly one of these two is set, or neither for a Note. */
  book?: BookRef
}

/* `as Props` rather than the `: Props` annotation the other components use. In THIS
   file Astro's inference leaves `Astro.props` as `Record<string, any>`, so the
   annotation fails on the two REQUIRED props — reproduced with the frontmatter intact
   and the template reduced to one <p>, so it is the checker and not this code. The
   cast asserts exactly what the annotation asserted; runtime is unaffected either way,
   and the call site in `[slug].astro` is still checked against `Props`. */
const {title, genre, date, body, topics, permalink, clip, book} = Astro.props as Props

/**
 * ── ONE CARD'S WORTH OF PROPS, COMPOSED HERE RATHER THAN IN THE CARD ─────────
 *
 * SourceCard takes `[author?] title [image?] [source?]` and branches on nothing; what
 * fills those slots is per-variant, so the mapping lives at the call site. That is
 * also what keeps the card reusable when a `presentation` wants one.
 *
 * `source` is the same ROLE in both — what published the thing — and different copy:
 * a Clipping's domain, a Book Note's "Publisher, Year". `filter(Boolean).join(', ')`
 * so a book with a publisher and no date reads "Houghton Mifflin" rather than
 * "Houghton Mifflin, ", and one with neither yields '' and the line is dropped.
 *
 * `clipRef.title` is the SOURCE ARTICLE's title, not the note's — "Still Holds:
 * Gall's Law" against the note's "Gall's Law When Complexity Comes Cheap". The two
 * are one word apart in the query and mixing them up would look plausible on screen.
 */
const sourceCard = clip
  ? {
      href: clip.clipUrl,
      title: clip.title,
      source: clip.publisher,
      image: clip.img,
      author: null,
    }
  : book
    ? {
        href: book.bookUrl,
        title: book.title,
        source: [book.publisher, formatYear(book.pubDate)].filter(Boolean).join(', '),
        image: null,
        author: book.author,
      }
    : null

/* Every field on both refs is optional in the schema, so a card with no destination
   or no title would render an empty box or a nameless link. Both are required props
   on SourceCard; this is where that requirement is enforced against real data. */
const hasCard = Boolean(sourceCard?.href && sourceCard.title)
---

{
  /*
  `h-entry` NEEDS ONE ROOT HOLDING BOTH `p-name` AND `e-content`, which Bridgy
  Publish reads to build a syndicated copy. On the article page that requirement
  is what forced <article> out to wrap the whole entry, because the hero sits
  between the title and the body and no element contained both.

  Here it costs nothing, because this element already wraps both bands. So a note
  is the easier place to get mf2 right, and this is what the article page had to
  reason its way to.

  NO `.entry` CLASS, and its absence is the point: `.entry` on the article page
  exists solely to be the flex column that scopes the hero's `order` swap. Nothing
  here reorders, so this is ordinary block flow and grid.

  The RSS and Related bands stay OUTSIDE this element, in the page. That is
  structural rather than tidy: note cards in the related band are exactly the thing
  a parser reads as `h-entry`, and nested inside this one they would be CHILD
  entries — i.e. replies.
*/
}
<article class="h-entry">
  {
    /* The title half moved to <EntryHeader> in 2026-09-02's extraction. A note
       passes NO hero, which is what makes its title gap 16 at every width rather
       than only at lg — see that component's header. This file had reasoned its
       way to the same value independently, which is what triggered the move. */
  }
  <EntryHeader
    title={title}
    genre={genre}
    date={date}
    permalink={permalink}
    basePath="/insights/"
  />

  <Band as="div" space="none" class="content-band">
    <Grid class="content-grid">
      {
        /* `.detail` is the prose flow role from base.css — the rhythm ramp, the
           measure cap and the thumbnail float all key off it. `e-content` is
           scoped HERE and not to the entry, so a syndicated copy carries the body
           without the eyebrow, the permalink or the topic list coming along. */
      }
      <div class="detail e-content">
        <Prose value={body} />
      </div>

      {
        /*
        ── THE RAIL, AND WHAT IT IS NOT ────────────────────────────────────────

        An <aside> nested in the entry's <article>, which is the documented meaning:
        content tangentially related to THIS note rather than to the site. Unnamed and
        inside <article> it maps to `generic`, so it adds no nameless landmark — which
        is why there is deliberately no `aria-label`.

        NOTHING PINS. DESIGN.md's rail table gives a Note "nothing pins — scrolls
        naturally", and Andy confirmed it (2026-09-01). That costs no code at all,
        which is the payoff of "stickiness is a property of a CHILD of the rail, never
        of the rail itself": the article page marks a child sticky, this one marks
        nothing, and there is no per-page branching or opt-out to remember.

        It also means the two invariants the article page carries — `.rail` must stay a
        stretched grid item, no `overflow` on any ancestor — do not bind here. Nothing
        would break if they were violated, because nothing is travelling.

        NO ELEMENT AT ALL FOR A PLAIN NOTE. The board draws an empty frame ("rail —
        reserved (span-3)", 434:711) and it is reserved by `--col-main` being a fixed
        span, not by anything occupying it. So a plain Note emits no <aside>, and the
        prose does not widen.
      */
      }
      {
        (hasCard || book?.img?.asset) && (
          <aside class="rail">
            {/*
              THE COVER IS OUTSIDE THE CARD, and the boards are explicit about it: the
              Book Note rail is a 231x348 image, 48, then a 231x154 card (451:1622).
              The Link card holds its thumbnail INSIDE; the Book card holds none.

              NO `aspect` — the cover renders at the asset's own shape. The board's
              231x348 is 0.664, and the live asset is 600x904, which is 0.664 to three
              places. So the board is drawing the image's natural ratio, and imposing
              one here would crop a book cover, which is the one image where the whole
              rectangle is the content.

              Its `altText` carries the real description ("Book cover for Proust and
              the Squid"), so this is NOT decorative and gets no `alt=""`.
            */}
            {book?.img?.asset && <SanityImage image={book.img} class="rail-cover" />}

            {hasCard && sourceCard && (
              <SourceCard
                href={sourceCard.href!}
                title={sourceCard.title!}
                author={sourceCard.author}
                source={sourceCard.source}
                image={sourceCard.image}
              />
            )}
          </aside>
        )
      }

      {
        /*
        ── TOPICS SIT BELOW THE BODY, AT EVERY WIDTH, NEVER IN THE RAIL ─────────

        A note's topics are in the main column on all three desktop boards and on
        both mobile ones (Andy, 2026-09-01: "topics never go in the sidebar for
        notes"). Placed at `--col-main` it is therefore always wide, and TopicList's
        container query gives it the inline run at every width WITHOUT this page
        asking for one. The rail form it never reaches is the same component in a
        231px column.

        AN <h2>, WHERE THE ARTICLE'S RAIL PASSES <h3>. The rail's headings are inside
        an <aside>, which signals apparatus; this one is a bare sibling of the body,
        and a Book Note's body already carries h2 sections. An h3 after them would
        read to a screen reader as a SUBSECTION of the last one — "Impressions >
        Topics" — which is false. h2 makes it a sibling instead: still apparatus
        rather than content, but navigationally honest. `as` costs nothing visually;
        TopicList pins the type either way.

        The `note-` prefix on the id namespaces it against the body's own h2 ids,
        which are slugified from heading text — a note titled "Topics" would collide.
      */
      }
      {
        topics.length > 0 && (
          <TopicList
            topics={topics}
            id="note-topics"
            basePath="/insights/"
            as="h2"
            microformats
            class="note-topics"
          />
        )
      }
    </Grid>
  </Band>
</article>

<style>
  /* ── Content band ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * No top padding — the title band's 16 is the entire gap, which is what puts the
   * rail's top edge level with the body's first line.
   *
   * The prose measure is the same 8 columns as an article's, and it does NOT widen
   * when the rail is empty: the plain Note board reserves the column rather than
   * reclaiming it ("rail — reserved (span-3)", 434:711). That falls out of
   * `--col-main` being a fixed span, so there is nothing to write for it — worth
   * saying only because "no rail, so widen the text" is the plausible wrong move.
   */
  .content-grid {
    padding-block-end: var(--rhythm-band);
  }

  .detail {
    grid-column: var(--col-main);
  }

  /* ── The rail ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * ── BELOW md IT DISPERSES; IT DOES NOT COLLAPSE ────────────────────────────
   *
   * `display: contents` erases the <aside>'s box while keeping its children, so below
   * md the cover and the card each become a grid item of <Grid> in their own right and
   * land in the main column, in order, between the body and the topics run. Both
   * mobile boards draw exactly that — body, 64, cover, 64, card, 64, Topics — and
   * every one of those 64s is <Grid>'s row gap rather than anything written here.
   *
   * The mechanism is the article page's, and this is the case its comment predicted:
   * "a Note's rail simply marks nothing sticky. Three behaviors, one mechanism."
   *
   * ── THE CHILDREN MUST CARRY A PLACEMENT TOKEN, AND IT IS EASY TO MISS ──────
   *
   * A dispersed child is an auto-placed grid item, so without `grid-column` it would
   * take ONE column of twelve — a 60px-wide book cover — rather than the full width.
   * `--col-rail` is the right token for both widths and not a coincidence: it is
   * `1 / -1` below md, which is what the dispersal wants, and at md these are ordinary
   * block children of `.rail` where the declaration is simply inert.
   *
   * `.rail` itself takes the same token, which is what puts it beside the prose at md.
   */
  .rail {
    display: contents;
  }

  .rail,
  .rail-cover,
  .source-card {
    grid-column: var(--col-rail);
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .rail {
      display: block;
    }

    /*
     * The cover-to-card gap, 48 on the Book Note board (image ends 348, card starts
     * 396). `rhythm-block` and not `rhythm-heading-minor` — both are 48, and DESIGN.md
     * says to pick by prefix and name rather than by value: what follows is a card,
     * not a heading.
     *
     * Only needed from md. Below it the two are separate grid items and the row gap
     * has already spaced them — at 64 rather than 48, which is what the mobile boards
     * draw.
     */
    .rail-cover + .source-card {
      margin-block-start: var(--rhythm-block);
    }
  }

  /* ── The topics run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * PLACEMENT ONLY. Everything the run looks like — the flex row, the colon, the
   * commas, the type, the link treatment — moved to TopicList, which decides between
   * the inline run and the stacked list from its own width rather than from a prop.
   * At `--col-main` it is always wide, so this page always gets the run.
   *
   * The 64 above comes from <Grid>'s row gap, since this is the next grid row.
   */
  .topic-list {
    grid-column: var(--col-main);
  }
</style>
